    {
      "id": "long-youtube",
      "source_type": "YOUTUBE",
      "source_title": "A two-part agent workflow experiment",
      "source_language": "en",
      "response_language": "ru",
      "chunk_size_chars": 2400,
      "content": "00:00 The speaker opens with a setup that many teams copy: one agent configuration with every available tool, skill, and instruction preloaded before any task begins. The argument for it sounds practical — the agent recognizes many commands, needs no discovery step, and never stops to ask which capability it should load. The speaker says this was their own recommendation for months, and the first half of the video explains why the approach felt productive.\n\n08:00 Early demonstrations make the large setup look convenient. The agent answers a scheduling question, edits a document, and runs a small script without any routing logic. Each demo is short, the instructions are familiar, and nothing visibly fails. The speaker notes that these demos share one property: the task matches a capability that was already loaded, so the agent never has to choose between many plausible tools. Viewers see a capable assistant rather than the cost of carrying every instruction.\n\n19:00 In repeated tasks on a fixed benchmark, the picture changes. Unrelated instructions consume the context window, and the agent misses explicit requirements more often because a plausible but irrelevant tool looks available. The speaker shows two runs with the same request: the preloaded agent picks a stale command and skips a required validation step, while the agent that loads one capability on demand follows the requirement. Accuracy falls as the bundle grows, even though every individual tool is useful in some other task.\n\n24:00 The benchmark itself is modest: forty repeated tasks, two agent configurations, and a fixed checker that records whether each explicit requirement survived. The speaker shows one failure in detail: an unrelated deployment instruction is treated as relevant, the agent runs an extra step, and the required schema check is skipped. Across the run, the preloaded configuration completed fewer tasks correctly, while the on-demand configuration spent slightly longer selecting its one capability. The speaker argues the trade is worth it because a wrong action costs more than a selection step.\n\n29:00 The final recommendation is to add a skill only after a concrete failure demonstrates the need, and to keep the default bundle small. The speaker repeats that globally preloading every skill performed worse in the experiment, and that the result is about task accuracy rather than latency. A short closing caveat says the benchmark covered tool-heavy coding and research tasks, so teams with mostly single-purpose flows may see a smaller difference.",
